{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788275979,"nonce":"35MM4Ey7o6Vdy9q/9DFvkIn9NL8=","message":"THE HIGHEST-VALUE PREDICATE IS THE CLOCK, AND THIS ISSUE NEVER MENTIONS IT.\nEvan, 2026-09-01, watching a bench pass: *\"you should focus on time not ticks\nsilly... drift will happen if you focus on ticks too much right?\"*\n\nEvery example in the body is a resource — `digest.resources.food_days`,\n`digest.power.gain_w`, `digest.colonists[*].mood_pct` — and the argument for\nthem (alerts lag, `food_days \u003c 3` leads) is right and unchanged. But\n`digest.time` already publishes `tick`, `hour`, `day_of_season`, `season` and\n`year`, so **`until {condition: {\"digest.time.hour\": {\"\u003e=\": 6}}}` is \"advance\nuntil dawn\" and falls out of this spec for free.** Nothing extra is needed. It\nis worth naming explicitly because a worker reading the body will build the\nresource cases and may never notice the clock is in the same field set.\n\n\n`advance` has no time-based halt today — `until` takes `letter | threat | alert\n| event`, all incident taps. So **the only way to advance a known amount of\ngame time is `--ticks N`**, and a tick-budgeted advance overshoots by up to\n`MaxTicksPerFrame(speed)`: 30 at Ultrafast. Those overshoots ACCUMULATE, and\nnothing re-anchors. M1 ran 27 advances; at Ultrafast that is up to ~810 ticks\nof drift, and an agent reasoning \"20,000 ticks have passed so it must be\nmorning\" is simply wrong by an amount it never sees.\n\n**A clock predicate cannot drift, because each call re-reads the real clock.**\n\"Advance until hour 6\" is self-correcting in a way \"advance 5,000 ticks\" is not.\n\n\n`overshoot_bound` exists to let 4.2 reason about how far an advance might\noverrun. It earned a post-mortem finding (M1 finding K — the bound was taken\nfrom the speed the advance EXITED at rather than the fastest it ran at), a fix,\nand a suite check that **cannot be automated**: `accept/s13-mod-surface.py`\ncheck 3.4 needs a human to press `3` in the bench window, because\n`DrainCommands` answers `busy` to every mid-advance command except `pause`.\nThat check has been skipped on every unattended run since it was written and was\nfirst satisfied today, by Evan pressing a key\n(`speed_changes: [{tick: 36926, from: Ultrafast, to: Superfast, by: \"external\"}]`,\nbound 30 from Ultrafast while the advance exited at Superfast — correct).\n\nAll of that apparatus is load-bearing only while **tick budgets are how the\nagent advances time**. With a clock predicate, per-call overshoot becomes a\nprecision footnote instead of a correctness property, because the next call\nre-anchors. The right fix for a permanently-manual check is not to automate it;\nit is to stop depending on what it guards.\n\n\nName `digest.time` as a first-class predicate target with a worked example, and\nsay plainly that a time predicate is the one that makes tick arithmetic\nunnecessary. One caution from the body's own open questions, which applies\nsharply here: \"whether a false-at-start predicate should halt immediately or\nrequire an edge.\" For a clock predicate the answer is clearly **edge** — `hour\n\u003e= 6` is true all afternoon, and \"wait until dawn\" must not return instantly at\n14:00. That is the difference between \"wait until\" and \"assert now\", and the\nclock is the case that makes it obvious.","files":null}]}